INT. OBSERVATORY LIBRARY - NIGHT

A circular room of dark shelves beneath a copper dome, the great telescope shrouded in canvas. A single oil lamp burns. Rain beats on the dome like fingers.

INSPECTOR HESTER VANDERMEER, fifties, stands at the reading table with her gloves still on. DR. LEOPOLD AUGUSTIN, seventies, the astronomer, sits in a leather chair wrapped in a plaid blanket. His assistant, MISS CLEMENTINE OYELOWO, twenties, stands behind the chair, one hand gripping its back.

HESTER
Dr. Augustin, you told the constable that you were alone in this room from nine until midnight.

LEOPOLD
I was.

HESTER
And Miss Oyelowo was at the cottage.

CLEMENTINE
I was.

HESTER
Then who lit the second lamp?

LEOPOLD
I beg your pardon?

HESTER
When the constable arrived, there were two lamps burning in this room. Now there is one.

LEOPOLD
I am an old man, Inspector. I may have forgotten.

HESTER
You forgot that you'd lit two lamps?

LEOPOLD
I forgot nothing. I only remember differently.

CLEMENTINE
Sir.

LEOPOLD
Hush, Clementine.

HESTER
Miss Oyelowo, if you have something to say, now is a very good time.

CLEMENTINE
I don't.

HESTER
Dr. Augustin, the telescope has been moved.

LEOPOLD
It rotates. That is its purpose.

HESTER
It has been moved toward the east window, Doctor. And the canvas was warm to the touch when I arrived.

LEOPOLD
Warm?

HESTER
As though someone had just been standing beneath it.

Leopold lifts his chin. Clementine's grip tightens.

CLEMENTINE
Inspector, it was me.

LEOPOLD
Clementine.

CLEMENTINE
It was me, Dr. Augustin. I'm sorry.

HESTER
Miss Oyelowo, you told me you were at the cottage.

CLEMENTINE
I lied.

HESTER
Why?

CLEMENTINE
Because he told me to.
